  "answer_text": "The Raman spectra were acquired at 20 positions along the X-axis from $37.0\\text{ mm}$ to $38.9\\text{ mm}$ in steps of $0.1\\text{ mm}$, with Y fixed at $25.248\\text{ mm}$. Prior to measurement, an autofocus procedure was performed at the starting position ($X=37.0$), which determined an optimal Z-height of $0.0037\\text{ mm}$. All spectra were collected using a laser power of $2\\%$ and an exposure time of $1.0\\text{ s}$, as these parameters provided a safe yet sufficient signal-to-noise ratio for the observed intensities.\n\nThe resulting spectra were compared to the reference file `T071_ref.csv` by interpolating each measured spectrum onto the common Raman shift axis ($200 \\text{--} 2000\\text{ cm}^{-1}$) of the reference. Similarity was quantified using both L2 distance and Cosine similarity.\n\nThe analysis revealed a significant jump in the similarity metrics between adjacent positions at index 14 and index 15 (corresponding to $X=38.4$ and $X=38.5\\text{ mm}$). Specifically, the largest change in cosine similarity ($|\\Delta \\text{Cos}| \\approx 0.0044$) and L2 distance ($\\Delta \\text{L2} \\approx 3052$) occurred at this boundary, indicating a distinct transition in the sample's chemical composition or physical properties at $X=38.4\\text{--}38.5\\text{ mm}$.\n\n```json\n{\"boundary_index\": 14}\n```",
